  <!-- § 08 granular: prices and volumes, currency. Inputs trade-dbie-tot-2012, trade-dbie-oil-volumes, trade-dbie-annual, trade-dbie-annual-inr -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">08</span><h2>Prices or volumes</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Indices, tonnes, and the rupee</span></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-tot" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3><span id="h-tot">–</span></h3>
            <div class="sub">Unit value (price) and quantum (volume) indices of exports and imports, 2012-13 = 100, financial years 2013-14 to {fyOf(T.tot.dates.at(-1))}. DGCI&amp;S indices.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-tot">–</div><div class="c">net terms of trade, latest year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>Export prices</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>Import prices</button>
            <button class="chip"><i class="dash"></i>Export volume</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#A3A9B4"></i>Import volume</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-tot" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, DBIE report 379 (DGCI&amp;S index numbers), base 2012-13 = 100. The export volume index for the latest year is withheld: see the note below.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>A unit value index tracks the average price per unit of the goods traded; a quantum index tracks how much was traded. When an export headline says "record", ask which one moved. The net terms of trade divide export prices by import prices: above 100 means a unit of exports buys more imports than it did in 2012-13.</p>
            <p>The indices cover only goods that are counted in units, so they move less tidily than the dollar totals. The export volume index in particular jumps from year to year in ways the value of exports does not; read it as rough.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {fyOf(T.tot.dates.at(-1))}</span>
            <p>Export prices stood at <b id="ex-t-ux">–</b> and import prices at <b id="ex-t-um">–</b> (2012-13 = 100). <span id="ex-t-withheld">–</span></p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:88px">
      <figure id="cb-crude" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3><span id="h-crude">–</span></h3>
            <div class="sub">Crude oil produced in India and crude oil imported, million tonnes, financial years 1972-73 to {fyOf(T.crude.dates.at(-1))}.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-crude">–</div><div class="c">imported share, latest year</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>Imported</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#5F6675"></i>Produced in India</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-crude" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, Handbook of Statistics on the Indian Economy (DBIE report 148), Ministry of Petroleum and Natural Gas data.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>Tonnes do not change with the price of oil, so this is the plate to read when the dollar bill for crude jumps. India's own oil fields have produced less each year for over a decade while refining has grown, so every extra litre of fuel the country uses is, in effect, imported.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {fyOf(T.crude.dates.at(-1))}</span>
            <p>Imported <b id="ex-cr-imp">–</b>, produced <b id="ex-cr-prod">–</b>.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>

    <div class="break" style="margin-top:88px">
      <figure id="cb-ccy" style="margin:0">
        <div class="plate-head">
          <div class="t">
            <h3><span id="h-ccy">–</span></h3>
            <div class="sub">Growth of merchandise exports over the previous year, in US dollars and in rupees, per cent, financial years 1991-92 to {fyOf(T.ccy.dates.at(-1))}.</div>
          </div>
          <div class="plate-val"><div class="v acc" id="pv-ccy">–</div><div class="c">years when rupee growth beat dollar growth</div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="legend">
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#2E4BD8"></i>In US dollars</button>
            <button class="chip"><i style="background:#C47A16"></i>In rupees</button>
          </div>
          <div class="plotzone"><div id="plot-ccy" style="position:relative"><div class="cb-tip"></div></div></div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: RBI, DBIE reports 649 (US$) and 432 (₹), DGCI&amp;S data. Growth computed from the annual values.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="svg">SVG</button><button data-dl="png">PNG</button><button data-dl="csv">CSV</button><a href="https://data.rbi.org.in" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="explain">
          <div class="ev">
            <span class="mk">How to read this</span>
            <p>The gap between the two lines is, roughly, how much the rupee lost against the dollar that year. A headline that reports exports in rupees will usually look better than one in dollars; neither is wrong, but only the dollar figure tells you whether India sold more to the world.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="now">
            <span class="mk">As of {fyOf(T.ccy.dates.at(-1))}</span>
            <p>Exports grew <b id="ex-cc-usd">–</b> in dollars and <b id="ex-cc-inr">–</b> in rupees.</p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 09 every cut: inputs trade-hs2-*-fy, trade-partners-*-fy inline; trade-hs4/6/8 and partner x chapter tables on request -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">09</span><h2>Look up any product or partner</h2><span class="line"></span><span class="unit">Financial-year totals: chapters, headings, tariff lines, partners</span></div>
    <div class="prose"><p>Whole-year totals for every chapter, heading and partner, and for every partner by chapter. (The table of every series, month by month, is at the end of the guide.) Choose a level and a flow, type a word or a code, and sort by any column.</p></div>
    <div class="break">
      <figure id="cb-every" style="margin:0">
        <div class="frame">
          <div class="xp-controls">
            <label>Level <select id="xp-level">
              <option value="hs2">HS chapters (2 digits)</option>
              <option value="hs4">HS headings (4 digits)</option>
              <option value="partners">Partner countries</option>
              <option value="cxc">Partner x chapter</option>
            </select></label>
            <label>Flow <select id="xp-flow"><option value="exports">Exports</option><option value="imports">Imports</option></select></label>
            <label class="xp-q">Find <input id="xp-q" type="search" placeholder="rice, 8517, Japan" autocomplete="off"></label>
            <span class="xp-n" id="xp-n">–</span>
          </div>
          <div class="ledger xp-table">
            <table>
              <thead><tr><th data-k="code">Code</th><th data-k="label">Description</th><th data-k="cur" class="n" id="xp-h-cur">–</th><th data-k="prev" class="n" id="xp-h-prev">–</th><th data-k="chg" class="n">Change</th></tr></thead>
              <tbody id="xp-body"></tbody>
            </table>
          </div>
          <div class="plotfoot">
            <span class="src">Source: Ministry of Commerce, TradeStat (DGCI&amp;S). US$ million. Financial years are April-to-March totals.</span>
            <span class="dl"><button data-dl="csv" id="xp-csv">CSV</button><a href="https://tradestat.commerce.gov.in/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Source</a></span>
          </div>
        </div>
      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

    <div class="prose xp-note"><p><b>Six- and eight-digit detail.</b> The 5,600 subheadings and 11,000 tariff lines, every month since January 2017, in dollars, rupees and quantity, are too large for these pages. They are published as files in the India Atlas data archive, one per year and flow, each on one HS edition; the headings above are their totals. <span id="xp-archive-link">{lines ? <a class="archive-link" href={lines.href}>{lines.tables.toLocaleString("en-IN")} files of subheadings and tariff lines, {lines.rows.toLocaleString("en-IN")} rows, as Parquet and CSV</a> : "The archive files are being prepared."}</span></p></div>

  <!-- § 10 what this measures: evergreen -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">10</span><h2>What this measures, and what it leaves out</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The figures count goods, valued in dollars at the exchange rates customs notifies every fortnight, at the moment they cross the border. They do not count services, which India sells well: software, business services and travel earn a large share of India's foreign income and make its overall deficit with the world much smaller than its deficit in goods. That is the subject of the external account guide.</p>
      <p>They do not say who made the goods or where the value was added. A phone exported from India may contain a Chinese screen, a Korean chip and an Indian assembly line; the full price counts as an Indian export and the parts count as imports. They also do not say where goods were finally used: oil shipped to a Dutch port may end up in Germany.</p>
      <p>Smuggled goods, informal border trade and goods that never pass customs are missing. So are values hidden by mis-invoicing. And a small share of trade every month has no country assigned ("unspecified"), which can swing a partner's figure.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- § 11 misreadings -->
  <section>
    <div class="sec-head"><span class="no">11</span><h2>How to misread these numbers</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <ul class="misread">
      <li><span class="no">1</span><div><h3>"Exports hit a record this month."</h3><p>Check three things first. Was the same month a year ago unusually weak, so that growth is flattered by the base? Did oil prices rise, lifting the value of petroleum-product exports without any more being shipped? And is it March, when exports peak every year? A record in dollars can be an ordinary month in tonnes.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">2</span><div><h3>"The trade deficit means India is losing money."</h3><p>A country that imports crude to refine and gold to save will run a goods deficit. What matters is whether services earnings, remittances and investment cover it, which is what the current account and the reserves show.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">3</span><div><h3>"India's deficit with China is money flowing to China."</h3><p>Partner balances describe where goods were declared to come from and go to. Much of what India buys from China becomes something India sells elsewhere: phones, medicines made from Chinese ingredients, solar panels. A bilateral deficit is not a measure of dependence or loss on its own.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">4</span><div><h3>"Gold imports fell, so Indians are buying less gold."</h3><p>Imports are measured in dollars. When the gold price rises sharply, Indians often buy fewer tonnes but pay more; when it falls they may buy more weight for less money. Look at the quantity, in kilograms, before concluding anything about demand.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">5</span><div><h3>"Exports grew 12% in rupees, so exporters did well."</h3><p>If the rupee fell 8% against the dollar that year, most of that growth is the exchange rate. The dollar figure tells you whether the world bought more from India.</p></div></li>
      <li><span class="no">6</span><div><h3>"This tariff line jumped tenfold, so a new industry was born."</h3><p>At 8 digits, codes are rewritten every few years and a line can absorb goods that used to be counted elsewhere. Before reading a jump at the tariff-line level, check the 4-digit heading it belongs to.</p></div></li>
    </ul>
  </section>

